WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.720
I hear from lots of
people every day

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:07.280
who are concerned about how
their diet is affecting their health.

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:09.960
They need answers
based on facts.

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:10.920
In other words,

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:12.920
in the peer-reviewed
medical literature,

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:15.720
and that is what
I’m here for.

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:19.000
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:21.800
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:25.000
Today we take a deep dive
into drugs and supplements

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:27.920
that don’t work as advertised.

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:32.480
And we start with a deceptive
supplement called Prevagen.

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:33.840
Over the last twenty years or so,

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:37.560
Big Pharma has invested more
than a half trillion dollars

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:41.840
into dementia treatment research,
but so far to little avail.

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:44.240
In light of this,
many have turned to supplements.

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:49.080
An AARP commissioned survey found
that 36% of those 74 years and older

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:53.400
take a supplement for brain health,
to the tune of billions of dollars a year.

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:55.480
The most commonly marketed
brain supplement

00:00:55.480 --> 00:01:00.240
was one I’d frankly
never heard of before: Prevagen.

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:04.040
Prevagen contains a protein
derived from a luminescent jellyfish

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:08.440
the company claims "has been
clinically shown to improve memory."

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:09.600
According to the company website,

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:11.960
“A landmark double-blind
and placebo controlled trial

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:14.200
demonstrated Prevagen
improved short-term memory,

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:17.320
learning, and delayed recall
over 90 days.”

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:20.160
But when you actually pull up the study,

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:25.600
not only did Prevagen fail to improve
memory, learning, or recall over placebo,

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:27.240
it failed to show a significant improvement

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:31.280
in any of the nine
measured cognitive tasks tested.

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:34.680
As an inquiry into Prevagen published by
the Center for Science in the Public Interest

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:39.360
was titled, “How Can This Memory Supplement
Flunk Its One Trial

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:42.080
and Still Be Advertised as Effective?”

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:46.320
And not just as effective but the #1
pharmacist recommended brand.

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:50.160
Considering the lack of sound
clinical evidence, how is that possible?

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:54.800
Presumably, they’re just as blitzed with
the same kind advertising as everyone else.

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:58.200
It’s no surprise the supplement
didn’t do anything

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:01.040
since the company’s own studies
showed the jellyfish protein

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:03.920
was rapidly digested
by stomach enzymes.

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:08.760
Of course, that didn’t stop them from
raking in more than $20 million a year.

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:12.280
Claiming the “marketers of Prevagen
preyed on the fears of older consumers

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:15.800
experiencing age-related memory loss,”
the Federal Trade Commission

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:19.960
and New York State’s Attorney General
filed a consumer protection complaint,

00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:23.960
charging the company with making false
and unsubstantiated claims.

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:29.760
The AARP weighed in, accusing the company
of “deceiving millions of aging Americans.”

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:34.160
In a move straight out of
the Big Pharma aducanumab playbook,

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:39.560
the company went back and conducted
more than 30 post hoc analyses of the data

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:43.760
and found a few positive findings
on a few tasks for some subgroups.

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:46.200
This cherry-picking
of subgroups after-the-fact

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:50.200
is a classic example of manipulation
sometimes called “p-hacking”

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:52.520
or “data dredging”
that can be described

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:56.520
as “placing a bet on a horse
after watching the race.”

00:02:56.520 --> 00:03:00.720
The makers of Prevagen settled a class action
lawsuit in 2020 with the FTC

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:04.200
over deceptive business practices
and false advertising.

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:07.440
Remarkably, though, the settlement allows them
to continue to market it

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:09.360
with the court-approved
disclaimer that claims

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:13.160
are “based on a clinical study
of subgroups of individuals …”

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:16.200
But with the amount of slicing
and dicing of data they did,

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:18.480
the chances of finding
at least one false positive

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:21.680
was estimated at 80 percent.

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:25.080
Prevagen may be more than
just a waste of money.

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:27.240
The manufacturer was cited
for failing to report

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:29.880
more than a thousand adverse events
related by consumers

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:31.840
to the Food and
Drug Administration (FDA).

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:34.360
Regulators can’t have it both ways, though.

00:03:34.360 --> 00:03:39.520
If the jellyfish protein is digested away,
how can it pose a safety risk?

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:44.800
Because dietary supplements are too often
adulterated with contaminants.

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:48.400
FDA inspectors specifically cited
Prevagen’s manufacturing facilities

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:51.640
for “objectionable conditions or practices.”

00:03:51.640 --> 00:03:55.880
A 2019 survey by Pew
found that more than half believed

00:03:55.880 --> 00:03:59.680
that the Food and Drug Administration
requires supplements be tested for safety,

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:02.400
but that isn’t true.

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:06.320
One study of 24 supplements sold
as cognitive performance boosters

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:08.720
found that most claimed
an ingredient on the label

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:10.600
that wasn’t actually in the supplement,

00:04:10.600 --> 00:04:13.400
and worse, 38 percent contained ingredients

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:16.880
not allowed in supplements
like prohibited drugs.

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:19.840
Another study of a dozen so-called
“brain health supplements”

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:22.680
similarly found 8 out of 12 were misbranded

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:24.880
(missing an ingredient
promised on the label),

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:27.000
and 10 out of 12
were deemed adulterated

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:30.080
(containing unlisted compounds,
for example caffeine

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:34.760
in a product that explicitly highlighted
all-caps ‘‘DECAFFEINATED’’ on the label).

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:39.520
Only 1 out of 12 was certiﬁed
to contain what it said it did.

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:41.640
The bottom line for Prevagen:

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:45.320
there is no acceptable evidence
that it's effective.

00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:48.880
Patients should be advised
not to take it.

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:49.960
In our next story,

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:51.640
we look at the safety
and efficacy

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:55.480
of two of the newest
Alzheimer’s drug treatments.

00:04:55.480 --> 00:04:58.920
Aducanumab, sold as Aduhelm,
is the first new drug

00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:02.100
approved for Alzheimer’s treatment
in nearly 20 years.

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:07.000
It involves getting IV infusions of an antibody
that targets the amyloid plaque protein,

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:10.640
which represents a core brain pathology
in the disease.

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:13.680
After a phase 1 study found
no change in the placebo group,

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:18.000
but a dramatic reduction in amyloid plaque
burden at different doses of the drug,

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:21.440
they jumped straight to phase 3:
two randomized controlled trials

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:25.240
to put it to the test
on thousands of Alzheimer’s patients.

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:27.840
Unfortunately, both trials
were stopped early

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:31.240
after a “futility analysis”
deemed them failures,

00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:35.400
as cognition and function continued
to decline in the treatment groups.

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:38.160
That shouldn’t be surprising
since over the last 20 years

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:41.480
there have been more than
20 amyloid-targeting therapies

00:05:41.480 --> 00:05:44.920
that have universally failed
to offer meaningful cognitive beneﬁt

00:05:44.920 --> 00:05:46.920
for people with Alzheimer’s disease.

00:05:46.920 --> 00:05:50.360
In fact, a 2022 investigation
into a seminal paper

00:05:50.360 --> 00:05:52.640
implicating amyloid as a causal factor

00:05:52.640 --> 00:05:56.840
in Alzheimer's alleged
“shockingly blatant” data fraud,

00:05:56.840 --> 00:06:00.720
which has further tarnished
the reputation of the amyloid theory.

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:03.840
So, no surprise Aduhelm flopped,

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:08.440
but what happened next
was surprising indeed.

00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:13.480
Just when the drug’s prospects seemed dead,
Biogen, the drug company that developed it,

00:06:13.480 --> 00:06:17.040
launched a secret campaign,
code-named “Project Onyx,”

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:19.840
to use back channels to convince
the Food and Drug Administration

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:21.840
to approve it anyway.

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:25.840
They went back through the data and found
the subset of patients in one of the trials

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:30.400
that had a 0.39 increase on an
18-point scale of cognitive function.

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:34.080
This was well below the minimal standard
for clinical significance

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:38.320
(judged to be at least 1 or 2 full points),
and the same subset,

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:43.440
at the same dose, in the other study
they funded failed to find even that effect.

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:46.720
This kind of post-hoc,
after-the-fact data dredging

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:49.160
is like “firing a shotgun at a barn

00:06:49.160 --> 00:06:51.520
and then painting a target
around the bullet holes,”

00:06:51.520 --> 00:06:54.320
as one FDA committee
statistician put it.

00:06:54.320 --> 00:06:58.520
FDA reviewers thoroughly
demolished Biogen’s re-analysis,

00:06:58.520 --> 00:07:01.280
concluding “there is no compelling
substantial&nbsp;evidence

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:04.000
of treatment effect&nbsp; or disease slowing … ”

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:08.760
The American Academy of Neurology noted
that 35 percent of those getting aducanumab

00:07:08.760 --> 00:07:12.840
suffered swelling or bleeding in the brain
within the first four months of treatment—

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:16.880
one in three—ten times more than
those in the placebo group.

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:21.840
So, not surprisingly, not a single member
of the FDA expert advisory panel

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:28.160
voted in favor of approval (10 out of 11
voted against with 1 “uncertain”).

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:31.520
Yet the FDA ignored
its own advisory committee,

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:36.480
and in 2021 approved
the drug anyway.

00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:40.440
The FDA admitted the submitted data
“left residual uncertainties

00:07:40.440 --> 00:07:44.240
regarding clinical benefit” and so
couldn’t give it the standard approval,

00:07:44.240 --> 00:07:47.240
which involves a designation
that the drug is safe and effective.

00:07:47.240 --> 00:07:50.360
Instead, they used an
“accelerated approval” pathway

00:07:50.360 --> 00:07:53.680
that allows drugs to be accepted
on the basis of surrogate markers,

00:07:53.680 --> 00:07:56.120
in this case amyloid clearance.

00:07:56.120 --> 00:08:00.520
But more than two dozen other
amyloid-targeting treatments flopped.

00:08:00.520 --> 00:08:04.160
And despite convincing plaque reduction,
there were no clinical improvements

00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:07.040
or even worsening of the disease.

00:08:07.040 --> 00:08:12.120
The FDA approval of aducanumab proved
to be one of the most controversial

00:08:12.120 --> 00:08:13.480
in recent memory.

00:08:13.480 --> 00:08:17.720
The approval despite near unanimous
opposition by its own expert committee

00:08:17.720 --> 00:08:21.120
is virtually unprecedented, leading three
of the committee members

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:23.720
to resign in protest,
one of them called it

00:08:23.720 --> 00:08:27.720
“probably the worst drug approval decision
in recent US history.”

00:08:27.720 --> 00:08:32.320
The response from the scientific community
may best be summed up by a commentary

00:08:32.320 --> 00:08:36.280
written by the head of the American
Geriatrics Society entitled,

00:08:36.280 --> 00:08:39.160
“My head just exploded …”

00:08:39.160 --> 00:08:43.680
The FDA did stipulate that Biogen
would have to run a “post-marketing” trial

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:46.280
proving that the drug
actually has some benefit,

00:08:46.280 --> 00:08:49.280
but has until 2030 to report the results.

00:08:49.280 --> 00:08:55.160
Meanwhile, Biogen is charging
$56,000 per person per year for the drug,

00:08:55.160 --> 00:08:59.200
with out-of-pocket expenses estimated
at up to $11,000 annually.

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:01.880
And this doesn’t include
the costs of IV administration

00:09:01.880 --> 00:09:06.400
or the sequential $4,000
to $7,000 MRI scans

00:09:06.400 --> 00:09:10.280
needed to monitor
for the brain bleeds and swelling.

00:09:10.280 --> 00:09:13.320
Yeah, the risks far exceed
any likely benefits,

00:09:13.320 --> 00:09:17.040
but shouldn’t patients and their families
be able to make up their own minds?

00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:20.360
The problem is that
the FDA stamp of approval implies

00:09:20.360 --> 00:09:22.120
aducanumab is safe and effective,

00:09:22.120 --> 00:09:25.600
when at the current time
it appears to be neither.

00:09:25.600 --> 00:09:29.400
The official position statement of the
American Medical Directors Association,

00:09:29.400 --> 00:09:32.200
which specializes
is long-term care facilities,

00:09:32.200 --> 00:09:36.640
is that the drug is “likely to have
extraordinarily negative consequences”

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:40.240
for the millions living
with dementia in this country.

00:09:40.240 --> 00:09:45.640
The European equivalent of the FDA does not
seem as easily swayed by Big Pharma,

00:09:45.640 --> 00:09:48.280
recommending refusal of the drug.

00:09:48.280 --> 00:09:50.440
I mean, that’s the whole point
of drug regulation,

00:09:50.440 --> 00:09:53.840
as one of the former FDA committee
members who resigned put it:

00:09:53.840 --> 00:09:57.000
a responsibility to help vulnerable
patients and their families,

00:09:57.000 --> 00:10:00.680
not just from sketchy drugs
but also from false hopes.

00:10:00.680 --> 00:10:04.200
A congressional investigation concluded
FDA approval of aducanumab

00:10:04.200 --> 00:10:07.720
was “rife with irregularities,”
raising “serious concerns

00:10:07.720 --> 00:10:13.320
about FDA’s lapses in protocol
and Biogen’s disregard of efficacy.”

00:10:13.320 --> 00:10:17.200
Of course, that didn’t stop the FDA
from their 2023 accelerated approval

00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:21.600
of a similar antibody,
lecanemab (sold as Lequembi),

00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:26.240
of similar questionable
efficacy and safety.

00:10:26.240 --> 00:10:28.600
Did you know that
there's little guarantee

00:10:28.600 --> 00:10:31.160
that a dietary supplement
will actually contain

00:10:31.160 --> 00:10:33.560
what is advertised
on the packaging?

00:10:33.560 --> 00:10:35.320
Here’s the story.

00:10:35.320 --> 00:10:39.600
In a video I did a few years ago,
I talked about how press releases

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:43.080
about the latest science coming
out of even top medical centers

00:10:43.080 --> 00:10:46.400
were filled with
overstatements and omissions.

00:10:46.400 --> 00:10:49.760
Medical journal press releases
suffered from similar problems,

00:10:49.760 --> 00:10:52.360
and press releases from the
drug companies themselves

00:10:52.360 --> 00:10:56.560
were no different, filled with
hype and exaggeration.

00:10:56.560 --> 00:11:00.440
What about press releases
issued by the supplements industry?

00:11:00.440 --> 00:11:02.520
Not even the companies
that published the studies,

00:11:02.520 --> 00:11:03.880
but like trade organizations

00:11:03.880 --> 00:11:08.240
for the multi-billion-dollar
dietary supplement industry?

00:11:08.240 --> 00:11:13.800
Researchers found that 100% of
supplements industry press releases

00:11:13.800 --> 00:11:18.240
contained "spin," meaning strategies
to hype or denigrate findings

00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:21.400
to distort the results
of clinical studies.

00:11:21.400 --> 00:11:25.080
And here's the crazy part:
industry press releases advocated

00:11:25.080 --> 00:11:27.920
supplement use in response
to more than 90%

00:11:27.920 --> 00:11:31.854
of even the studies that
reported no benefit or harm.

00:11:31.880 --> 00:11:34.880
A study comes out showing a
supplement is actually bad for you,

00:11:34.880 --> 00:11:37.480
and the vast majority of
independent institutions like

00:11:37.480 --> 00:11:40.400
the National Institutes of Health
or mainstream media stories

00:11:40.400 --> 00:11:44.680
are like "don't take it."
Duh. But the industry trade

00:11:44.680 --> 00:11:49.160
organizations are like, "Harm?
Go for it! Buy an extra bottle."

00:11:49.160 --> 00:11:52.200
It is, therefore, likely that the
propagation of the spin-enriched

00:11:52.200 --> 00:11:56.080
industry press releases contributes
to the ongoing, and even burgeoning,

00:11:56.080 --> 00:11:59.960
enthusiasm for use of supplements
in the face of accumulating evidence

00:11:59.960 --> 00:12:04.560
of most of their ineffectiveness
and, in some cases, harm.

00:12:04.560 --> 00:12:09.000
In some cases, people are paying
to make themselves sick.

00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:13.240
Sadly, the science may not even
matter to many supplement users.

00:12:13.240 --> 00:12:17.480
Most dietary supplement users said
that they would be minimally influenced

00:12:17.480 --> 00:12:20.840
by independent, taxpayer-funded
studies contradicting

00:12:20.840 --> 00:12:23.880
the efficacy claims of
supplement manufacturers.

00:12:23.880 --> 00:12:27.160
Only a minority of users said they
would stop taking a supplement

00:12:27.160 --> 00:12:31.520
if public health authorities stated
that it was just a waste of money.

00:12:31.520 --> 00:12:37.200
The iconic image of the snake oil
salesman was actually a real dude:

00:12:37.200 --> 00:12:41.920
Clark "the Rattlesnake King" Stanley
in 1916.

00:12:41.920 --> 00:12:46.880
Turns out that Stanley's snake oil,
in fact, contained no snake oil at all

00:12:46.880 --> 00:12:50.760
but rather just like spicy
mothballs and turpentine,

00:12:50.760 --> 00:12:54.160
prosecuted under the newly enacted
Pure Food and Drug Act,

00:12:54.160 --> 00:12:59.000
and ultimately fined a
lofty sum of $20 bucks.

00:12:59.000 --> 00:13:03.400
And a century later, here we are
with snake oil coming out our ears,

00:13:03.400 --> 00:13:08.160
a vast number of supplements marketed
and sold without routine oversight.

00:13:08.160 --> 00:13:10.880
Now, the supplement industry
bristles at such talk, saying that

00:13:10.880 --> 00:13:14.640
the supplement industry is
regulated to protect consumers.

00:13:14.640 --> 00:13:18.000
And it's true there are definitely
laws on the books, but the ability

00:13:18.000 --> 00:13:21.800
of regulators to successfully carry
out this mission is hampered

00:13:21.800 --> 00:13:24.200
by the sheer number of products.

00:13:24.200 --> 00:13:27.880
The FDA estimates there are more
than 85,000 dietary supplement

00:13:27.880 --> 00:13:30.600
products currently available
in the U.S. alone.

00:13:30.600 --> 00:13:34.360
So there's the sheer number,
the under reporting of adverse

00:13:34.360 --> 00:13:37.480
side effects, the difficulty
of successfully prosecuting

00:13:37.480 --> 00:13:40.720
cases against offenders,
and the ease with which

00:13:40.720 --> 00:13:43.880
suppliers can rebrand products
removed from the shelves

00:13:43.880 --> 00:13:47.520
have led to a largely
unregulated environment.

00:13:47.520 --> 00:13:50.200
In the real world, there is little
guarantee that a supplement

00:13:50.200 --> 00:13:53.560
will even contain what is
advertised on the packaging and

00:13:53.560 --> 00:13:57.520
not contain unlisted ingredients,
potentially leading to significant

00:13:57.520 --> 00:14:03.080
harms, resulting in an estimated
23,000 ER visits every year.

00:14:03.080 --> 00:14:07.320
Now, of course, prescription drugs
don't just sicken but kill many more,

00:14:07.320 --> 00:14:10.360
making them perhaps the
third leading cause of death,

00:14:10.360 --> 00:14:13.840
wiping out more than
100,000 Americans, but still,

00:14:13.840 --> 00:14:17.680
the less death and disability,
the better.

00:14:17.680 --> 00:14:19.720
Dietary supplements
are often adulterated

00:14:19.720 --> 00:14:22.400
with undeclared pharmaceuticals,
which could represent

00:14:22.400 --> 00:14:26.600
an enormous risk to consumers, most
often found in erectile dysfunction,

00:14:26.600 --> 00:14:30.200
weight loss, diabetes, and high
blood pressure supplements.

00:14:30.200 --> 00:14:32.080
But wait a second.
What about

00:14:32.080 --> 00:14:35.200
supplement manufacturers
who say they have independent,

00:14:35.200 --> 00:14:38.520
third-party certification of purity?

00:14:38.520 --> 00:14:42.760
There is a practice called
"dry labbing," a dirty little secret

00:14:42.760 --> 00:14:45.920
of the supplements industry,
where quality assurance labs

00:14:45.920 --> 00:14:48.480
just rubber stamp fake documents.

00:14:48.480 --> 00:14:54.480
And if you think that's outrageous,
check out the story of BMPEA.

00:14:54.480 --> 00:14:58.680
A researcher at Harvard published
a paper replicating prior research

00:14:58.680 --> 00:15:03.600
from the FDA detecting a designer
amphetamine-like stimulant,

00:15:03.600 --> 00:15:05.400
beta-methylphenylethylamine,

00:15:05.400 --> 00:15:08.920
in various U.S. supplements
sold in the United States.

00:15:08.920 --> 00:15:12.440
In response, one of the offenders,
Hi-Tech Pharmaceuticals,

00:15:12.440 --> 00:15:14.760
manufacturers of weight
loss supplements like

00:15:14.760 --> 00:15:17.400
Black Widow and Yellow Scorpion,

00:15:17.400 --> 00:15:20.920
sued the Harvard researcher
for libel, slander, and product

00:15:20.920 --> 00:15:27.160
disparagement, originally to the
tune of $200 million in damages.

00:15:27.160 --> 00:15:30.600
This was all documented
in a piece by STAT,

00:15:30.600 --> 00:15:32.840
which is an excellent source
of medical journalism

00:15:32.840 --> 00:15:34.720
that I recommend.

00:15:34.720 --> 00:15:37.400
The head of Hi-Tech openly
admitted that he was

00:15:37.400 --> 00:15:40.200
"hoping that we were
able to silence this guy."

00:15:40.200 --> 00:15:44.640
And while ultimately unsuccessful in
court, Hi-Tech's lawsuit effectively

00:15:44.640 --> 00:15:47.000
sent a warning to other researchers.

00:15:47.000 --> 00:15:49.360
In fact, Hi-Tech's CEO is
attributed as saying,

00:15:49.360 --> 00:15:53.240
he "hope[s] that the long and costly
legal battle will scare away

00:15:53.240 --> 00:15:57.440
other academics from investigating
the supplement industry."

